(   1. Core Elements of Dimensioning:) Tj
T*
(   - Dimension Line: A thin continuous line \(Type B\) indicating the extent and direction of a) Tj
T*
(   dimension, terminated by arrowheads.) Tj
T*
(   - Extension Line \(Projection Line\): Extends outwards from the object feature by 2 to 3 mm) Tj
T*
(   beyond the dimension line, leaving a gap of 1 mm from the feature contour.) Tj
T*
(   - Arrowheads: Terminate dimension lines. Standard proportions are 3:1 \(length:width\), closed) Tj
T*
(   and solidly filled with black ink/lead.) Tj
T*
(   - Leader Line: An inclined thin line leading from a note or dimension to a feature, terminating) Tj
T*
(   in an arrowhead \(touching an outline\) or dot \(within a surface\), with a horizontal landing of) Tj
T*
(   2-3 mm at the note.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Comparison of Dimensioning Systems:) Tj
T*
(   - Aligned System:) Tj
T*
(   * Placement: Dimension values are placed parallel to the dimension line and positioned slightly) Tj
T*
(   above it \(never touching the line\).) Tj
T*
(   * Reading Direction: Can be read from the bottom of the drawing sheet or from the right-hand) Tj
T*
(   edge.) Tj
T*
(   * Application: Predominantly used in civil, architectural, and structural drawings.) Tj
T*
(   - Unidirectional System:) Tj
T*
(   * Placement: All dimension figures are oriented horizontally, breaking the dimension line near) Tj
T*
(   its center.) Tj
T*
(   * Reading Direction: Read exclusively from the bottom of the drawing sheet, regardless of the) Tj
T*
(   angle of the feature.) Tj
T*
(   * Application: Universally favored in mechanical engineering, aerospace, and CAD practice due) Tj
T*
(   to easier legibility on large sheets.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Golden Rules of Dimensioning:) Tj
T*
(   - Never use centerlines or visible outlines as dimension lines.) Tj
T*
(   - Place dimensions outside views wherever practical.) Tj
T*
(   - Avoid redundant over-dimensioning; every feature must be dimensioned exactly once.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 7:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Discuss the rules for lettering in engineering drawing according to BIS SP 46 \(ISO 3098\), detailing single-stroke vertical gothic letters, heights, and spacing ratios.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Nature and Definition of Single-Stroke Lettering:) Tj
T*
(   Single-stroke lettering refers to letterforms where the line thickness of each character stroke) Tj
T*
(   matches the nominal line width \(d\) produced in a single continuous movement of the pen or) Tj
T*
(   pencil, without double-line outlining or shading.) Tj
T*
(   Letters must be uniform, legible, and suitable for microfilming and electronic scanning.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Classification:) Tj
T*
(   - Vertical Gothic: Characters drawn upright at 90 degrees to baseline \(mandatory for most) Tj
T*
(   institutional and manufacturing standards\).) Tj
T*
(   - Inclined \(Italic\) Gothic: Characters sloped at 75 degrees forward to the horizontal baseline.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Standard Height Progression \(ISO 3098\):) Tj
T*
(   Standard nominal heights \(h\) follow a geometric ratio of sqrt\(2\):) Tj
T*
(   h = 1.8 mm, 2.5 mm, 3.5 mm, 5.0 mm, 7.0 mm, 10.0 mm, 14.0 mm, 20.0 mm.) Tj
T*
(   - Main drawing titles: 7.0 mm or 10.0 mm.) Tj
T*
(   - Subtitles and section headings: 5.0 mm.) Tj
T*
(   - Dimensions, notes, tolerance values: 3.5 mm.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET

