(>> Solution to Question 8:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Define Representative Fraction \(RF\), classify scales into full, reducing, and enlarging scales with engineering examples, and derive Length of Scale \(LOS\).) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Definition of Representative Fraction \(RF\):) Tj
T*
(   Representative Fraction is the dimensionless mathematical ratio of the linear dimension of an) Tj
T*
(   element as drawn on paper to the actual physical linear dimension of that same element:) Tj
T*
(   RF = \(Length on Drawing\) / \(Actual Physical Length\).) Tj
T*
(   Both numerator and denominator MUST be converted into identical units \(e.g. millimeters\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Engineering Classification of Scales:) Tj
T*
(   - Full Size Scale \(RF = 1:1\): The drawing size is identical to actual part size \(e.g., standard) Tj
T*
(   machine bolts, electronic IC packaging\).) Tj
T*
(   - Reducing Scale \(RF < 1\): Used when physical objects are too large for drawing sheets:) Tj
T*
(   * 1:2, 1:5, 1:10 \(Machine components, engine blocks\).) Tj
T*
(   * 1:50, 1:100, 1:200 \(Architectural floor plans, structural layouts\).) Tj
T*
(   * 1:1,000, 1:10,000, 1:50,000 \(Topographical survey maps, civil highways\).) Tj
T*
(   - Enlarging Scale \(RF > 1\): Used for tiny, micro-scale features:) Tj
T*
(   * 2:1, 5:1, 10:1 \(Watch movements, precision gears, semiconductor micro-vias\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Mathematical Formulation for Length of Scale \(LOS\):) Tj
T*
(   LOS is the physical length of the scale line to be constructed on the drawing sheet:) Tj
T*
(   LOS = RF x Maximum Length to be Measured \(ML\).) Tj
T*
(   Example: To measure up to 5 meters with RF = 1:50:) Tj
T*
(   LOS = \(1 / 50\) x 5 m = \(1 / 50\) x 500 cm = 10 cm = 100 mm.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 9:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Construct a plain scale of RF = 1:40 to show meters and decimeters, capable of measuring up to 6 meters. Provide full mathematical calculations and step-by-step drafting procedure to mark a distance of 4.6 meters.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Mathematical Calculations:) Tj
T*
(   - Given: RF = 1 / 40.) Tj
T*
(   - Maximum Length to measure \(ML\) = 6 meters = 600 cm.) Tj
T*
(   - Length of Scale \(LOS\) = RF x ML = \(1 / 40\) x 600 cm = 15.0 cm \(150 mm\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Step-by-Step Drafting Procedure:) Tj
T*
(   - Step 1: Draw a horizontal line AB of length 150 mm. Draw a parallel line 10 mm above it to) Tj
T*
(   form a scale rectangle of 150 mm x 10 mm.) Tj
T*
(   - Step 2: Divide the 150 mm length into 6 equal primary divisions using the acute-angle line) Tj
T*
(   division method. Each division represents 1 meter \(150 mm / 6 = 25 mm per meter\).) Tj
T*
(   - Step 3: Label the divisions: Place the zero mark '0' at the end of the first division \(25 mm) Tj
T*
(   from left\). Number to the right from 0: 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 meters.) Tj
T*
(   - Step 4: Subdivide the leftmost 1-meter division \(from 0 to the left\) into 10 equal parts.) Tj
T*
ET

