(   [Problem Statement]: Describe the LIMITS command in AutoCAD, explaining its role in setting drawing boundaries, how to enable/disable limit checking, and its relationship with ZOOM ALL.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Purpose of the LIMITS Command:) Tj
T*
(   In AutoCAD, the drawing canvas is an infinite mathematical space. The LIMITS command defines an) Tj
T*
(   artificial two-dimensional boundary in World Coordinates that matches the intended physical) Tj
T*
(   drawing sheet format \(e.g., A3, A4\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Command Execution Sequence:) Tj
T*
(   - Command: LIMITS [Enter]) Tj
T*
(   - Prompt: Specify lower left corner or [ON/OFF] <0.0000, 0.0000>: 0,0 [Enter]) Tj
T*
(   - Prompt: Specify upper right corner <420.0000, 297.0000>: 420,297 [Enter] \(for standard ISO A3) Tj
T*
(   sheet in millimeters\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Limit Checking \(ON / OFF Mode\):) Tj
T*
(   - LIMITS -> ON: Enforces boundary validation. If a user attempts to click or enter coordinates) Tj
T*
(   outside the defined box \(e.g., -10, 50\), AutoCAD rejects the input with the message '**Point) Tj
T*
(   outside limits'.) Tj
T*
(   - LIMITS -> OFF: Allows unrestrained drawing anywhere in infinite space while preserving the) Tj
T*
(   defined boundary for grid display.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   4. Interaction with ZOOM ALL:) Tj
T*
(   - Executing ZOOM -> ALL \(Z -> Enter -> A -> Enter\) recalculates the display magnification to) Tj
T*
(   show the full extent of the drawing limits OR all drawn objects, whichever bounding box is) Tj
T*
(   larger.) Tj
T*
(   - If grid display is configured to 'Display grid beyond Limits' = OFF, the visual grid dots) Tj
T*
(   will appear exclusively inside the defined limits rectangle, visually framing the sheet.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 16:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Detail the navigation tools in AutoCAD: ZOOM options \(All, Extents, Window, Dynamic\), PAN, and mouse wheel shortcuts for viewport manipulation.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. ZOOM Command Variations:) Tj
T*
(   - ZOOM -> ALL \(Z [Enter] A [Enter]\): Displays the entire drawing area defined by sheet limits) Tj
T*
(   or the bounding box of all drawn objects, whichever is larger.) Tj
T*
(   - ZOOM -> EXTENTS \(Z [Enter] E [Enter]\): Calculates the minimum bounding box containing all) Tj
T*
(   visible geometry and zooms in/out to fill the graphics window completely.) Tj
T*
(   - ZOOM -> WINDOW \(Z [Enter] W [Enter]\): Prompts the user to pick two diagonal corners of a) Tj
T*
(   rectangular window, instantly magnifying the bounded area to fill the screen.) Tj
T*
(   - ZOOM -> PREVIOUS \(Z [Enter] P [Enter]\): Restores the exact prior viewport zoom and pan state) Tj
T*
(   \(stores up to 10 previous views\).) Tj
T*
(   - ZOOM -> REALTIME: Dragging cursor upward zooms in; dragging downward zooms out.) Tj
T*
(   - ZOOM -> OBJECT \(Z [Enter] O [Enter]\): Zooms to center and maximize a single selected object.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. PAN Tool Operations:) Tj
T*
(   - Function: Translates the viewing window in any direction without changing the drawing) Tj
T*
(   magnification or modifying object coordinates.) Tj
T*
(   - Command: PAN \(or 'P' [Enter]\) activates a hand cursor; click and drag to shift view.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET

