(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the methods of constructing an ellipse in engineering drawing and compare with AutoCAD's ELLIPSE command options \(Axis End, Center, Isocircle\).) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Manual Drafting Methods for Ellipse Construction:) Tj
T*
(   - Concentric Circles Method:) Tj
T*
(   * Draw two concentric circles with major axis diameter \(2a\) and minor axis diameter \(2b\)) Tj
T*
(   centered at O.) Tj
T*
(   * Divide both circles into 12 equal sectors by drawing radial lines at 30? increments.) Tj
T*
(   * From intersections on the outer circle, project vertical lines; from intersections on the) Tj
T*
(   inner circle, project horizontal lines.) Tj
T*
(   * The intersections of these orthogonal projectors form points on the true mathematical) Tj
T*
(   ellipse.) Tj
T*
(   - Trammel Method:) Tj
T*
(   * A straight strip of paper \(trammel\) with marked distances equal to semi-minor axis b and) Tj
T*
(   semi-major axis a. As trammel points slide along major and minor axes, the end point traces an) Tj
T*
(   ellipse.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. AutoCAD ELLIPSE Command Options:) Tj
T*
(   - Command: ELLIPSE [Enter]) Tj
T*
(   - Option 1: Axis, End \(Default\):) Tj
T*
(   * Prompts: 'Specify axis endpoint of ellipse' -> Pick point P1.) Tj
T*
(   * 'Specify other endpoint of axis' -> Pick point P2 \(defines major or minor axis length 2a\).) Tj
T*
(   * 'Specify distance to other axis' -> Enter distance b from axis midpoint to third point.) Tj
T*
(   - Option 2: Center \(C\):) Tj
T*
(   * Prompts: Pick center point C -> Specify endpoint of first semi-axis -> Specify length of) Tj
T*
(   second semi-axis.) Tj
T*
(   - Option 3: Isocircle \(I\) [Available only when ISODRAFT mode is active]:) Tj
T*
(   * Accurately constructs an isometric circle on the active isoplane by specifying center and) Tj
T*
(   radius.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 26:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Construct an engineering scale of chords to measure angles from 0 to 90 degrees in 5-degree increments. Derive the mathematical formula and describe how to construct an angle of 35 degrees using only a compass and scale of chords.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Theoretical Basis and Mathematical Derivation:) Tj
T*
(   - A scale of chords is a linear graphical scale that represents the chord lengths of a circle) Tj
T*
(   of reference radius R corresponding to subtended central angles theta.) Tj
T*
(   - In a circle of radius R, consider an isosceles triangle formed by two radii of length R) Tj
T*
(   separated by central angle theta.) Tj
T*
(   - Bisecting angle theta gives two right-angled triangles with half-angle \(theta / 2\).) Tj
T*
(   - The opposite half-chord length is R * sin\(theta / 2\).) Tj
T*
(   - Therefore, the total chord length C is:) Tj
T*
(   C = 2 * R * sin\(theta / 2\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Construction of the Scale of Chords \(0? to 90?\):) Tj
T*
(   - Step 1: Draw a quadrant of a circle of convenient radius R \(e.g. R = 100 mm\) with center O) Tj
T*
(   and perpendicular radii OA and OB \(angle AOB = 90?\).) Tj
T*
(   - Step 2: Draw chord AB. The true length of chord AB = 2 * 100 * sin\(45?\) = 141.42 mm.) Tj
T*
(   - Step 3: Divide the 90? circular arc AB into 18 equal parts of 5? each using a protractor or) Tj
T*
(   geometric bisector method.) Tj
T*
(   - Step 4: With A as center and compass radius equal to distance from A to each arc division) Tj
T*
ET

